{Your Caro-Kann main line, and the sacrifice that can punish one slip. 13-year-old Carlsen
against the Caro-Kann Classical. Moves 1 to 10 are exactly your repertoire. Study it from
both sides.} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8.
h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bf4 Ngf6 12. O-O-O {Opposite-side castling. In your
repertoire Black plays 12...Be7 and 13...O-O, as here.} 12... Be7 13. Ne4 Qa5 14. Kb1 O-O
15. Nxf6+ Nxf6 16. Ne5 Rad8 17. Qe2 {Now 17...c5 looks natural, but it loosens e6, a
sensitive square in the Caro-Kann. The engine preferred 17...Qb6.} 17... c5 18. Ng6
{Question: Black's last move weakened e6. Find the sacrifice. 18.Ng6! fxg6 19.Qxe6+ and
the light squares around the black king collapse. 21.Bxh6! and 22.Rxh6+ follow. The engine
rates White better, and in practice it is very hard to defend.} 18... fxg6 19. Qxe6+ Kh8
20. hxg6 Ng8 21. Bxh6 gxh6 22. Rxh6+ Nxh6 23. Qxe7 Nf7 24. gxf7 Kg7 25. Rd3 Rd6 26. Rg3+
Rg6 27. Qe5+ Kxf7 28. Qf5+ Rf6 29. Qd7# {Checkmate. Lesson for your Caro-Kann: keep e6
protected, and think twice about ...c5 while the white pieces are aimed at your king.} 1-0

